.TH "SKARN" "1" "2026-06-23" "skarn 0.17.0" "Skarn Manual"
.SH NAME
skarn \- AI coding session security scanner with built\-in session search
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBskarn\fR \fBcheck\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBassess\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBbaseline\fR \fIaudit|accept\fR \fIfile\fR [<fingerprint>] [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBsearch\fR \fIquery\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBrecent\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBrestore\fR \fIsession\fR
\fBskarn\fR \fBmessages\fR \fIsession\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBstats\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBtools\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBmcps\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBcmds\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBexport\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBguard\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBsetup\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBdoctor\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBcompletion\fR \fIshell\fR
\fBskarn\fR \fBserve\fR [options]
\fBskarn\fR \fBtaxonomies\fR
\fBskarn\fR \fBlicense\fR [<file>|\-] [options]
skarn \fB\-\-version\fR
skarn \fB\-\-help\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Skarn is an AI coding session security scanner with built\-in session search.
It detects leaked credentials and the attack patterns that cause or exploit
them in the local session transcripts of AI coding assistants (Claude Code,
Gemini CLI, Codex CLI, Cursor, and VS Code Copilot Chat), and it lets you
search, browse, and analyze those sessions.
.PP
Skarn is a single static binary. It scans locally and makes no network
connection by default; the only features that transmit data off the machine
are opt\-in and named explicitly in the SECURITY section below.
.PP
Skarn has two faces over one parsing engine. The recall commands (search,
recent, stats, and friends) are the daily\-use surface and never trigger a
scan. The check command is the security scanner: it runs the detection rules,
correlates attack chains, and computes a session risk score for CI/CD gating.
Every finding is mapped to MITRE ATLAS, the OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications
2025, and CWE; run skarn taxonomies to print the crosswalk and the pinned
framework versions.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBcheck\fR [options]
Scan AI sessions for leaked credentials and attack patterns.
.TP
\fBassess\fR [options]
Scan every AI session on this machine and print a friendly risk summary, with
an optional shareable redacted report. Free, no config, no license.
.TP
\fBbaseline\fR \fIaudit|accept\fR \fIfile\fR [<fingerprint>] [options]
Interactively triage findings into a committed baseline (audit), or record a
false\-positive fingerprint (accept).
.TP
\fBsearch\fR \fIquery\fR [options]
Search past AI sessions (literal text, or \-\-regex).
.TP
\fBrecent\fR [options]
List recent sessions.
.TP
\fBrestore\fR \fIsession\fR
Restore a session.
.TP
\fBmessages\fR \fIsession\fR [options]
Show the messages of a session.
.TP
\fBstats\fR [options]
Session analytics: tokens, models, tools, and timing (text, json, csv, or
html).
.TP
\fBtools\fR [options]
Show the tools used across sessions.
.TP
\fBmcps\fR [options]
Show the MCP calls made across sessions.
.TP
\fBcmds\fR [options]
Show the shell commands run across sessions.
.TP
\fBexport\fR [options]
Export sessions as text, json, ndjson, or html (redacted by default).
.TP
\fBguard\fR [options]
Real\-time pre\-execution hook for editor/agent integration; reads an event on
stdin and emits a verdict.
.TP
\fBsetup\fR [options]
Wire the skarn guard hook into detected AI coding agents (merge\-based,
reversible), then verify it with the guard self\-test.
.TP
\fBdoctor\fR [options]
Check whether skarn is actually protecting this machine: binary, license,
wired agent hooks, guard log, session stores, and the guard self\-test.
.TP
\fBcompletion\fR \fIshell\fR
Print a shell completion script for bash, zsh, or fish.
.TP
\fBserve\fR [options]
Start the local web UI (search, recent, stats, and a redacted scan view) bound
to 127.0.0.1.
.TP
\fBtaxonomies\fR
Print the standards crosswalk (MITRE ATLAS, OWASP LLM Top 10, CWE).
.TP
\fBlicense\fR [<file>|\-] [options]
Show the active license, or install one from a file (or stdin).
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Check options"
Control the security scan: which rules run, the scan window, output format,
and CI gating.
.TP
\fB\-\-rules\fR \fIpath\fR
Load a custom TOML rules file in addition to the bundled rules.
.TP
\fB\-\-ai\-rules\fR \fIpath\fR
Load an AI\-specific rules file.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-ai\-rules\fR
Disable the AI\-specific rules.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-default\-rules\fR
Disable the bundled detection rules.
.TP
\fB\-\-feed\fR \fIpath\fR
Apply a signed maintained\-feed bundle over the baseline rules. Requires a
Team license.
.TP
\fB\-\-update\-rules\fR
Fetch and verify the latest feed from the subscriber channel, then scan.
Requires a Team license.
.TP
\fB\-\-feed\-url\fR \fIurl\fR
Feed channel URL for \-\-update\-rules; falls back to $SKARN_FEED_URL.
Requires a Team license.
.TP
\fB\-\-offline\fR
Disable all network access (the maintained\-feed fetch).
.TP
\fB\-\-severity\fR \fIlevel\fR
Report only findings at or above this severity. One of: low, medium, high,
critical. The default is medium.
.TP
\fB\-\-canary\-file\fR \fIpath\fR
File of planted canary tokens, one per line; a match is a proven breach.
.TP
\fB\-\-check\-packages\fR
Flag typosquatted or URL\-sourced package installs found in sessions
(offline).
.TP
\fB\-\-check\-code\fR
Flag insecure code written by the assistant during the session.
.TP
\fB\-\-hours\fR \fIn\fR
Scan window in hours; 0 means no time limit. The default is 720.
.TP
\fB\-\-cli\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict the scan to one assistant's sessions. One of: claude, gemini, codex,
cursor, copilot.
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIpath\fR
Restrict the scan to sessions under this project path.
.TP
\fB\-\-session\fR \fIid\fR
Scan a single session by id.
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fIfmt\fR
Output format. ndjson is an ECS\-shaped event stream (one redacted finding per
line) for SIEM ingest. One of: text, json, sarif, ndjson. The default is text.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Verbose output.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-redact\fR
Show secrets in full instead of redacting them. Use with caution.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR
Disable colored output.
.TP
\fB\-\-policy\fR \fIpath\fR
Load a declarative TOML policy file (fail thresholds, required/forbidden
rules, baseline enforcement, profile); the \-\-fail\-on\-* and \-\-profile
flags override it. Requires a Team license.
.TP
\fB\-\-profile\fR \fIpreset\fR
Output profile. enterprise keeps high\-confidence findings and dedups them.
One of: enterprise. Requires an Enterprise license.
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-severity\fR \fIlevel\fR
Exit 1 if any finding is at or above this severity. One of: low, medium, high,
critical.
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-risk\fR \fI0\-100\fR
Exit 2 if the session risk score exceeds this threshold.
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-scan\-error\fR
Fail closed (exit 6) when the scan was incomplete: a tool source errored out
of discovery, or a session's parse failed. Off by default (a partial scan
still reports, with a stderr warning).
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\fR \fIpath\fR
Suppress findings recorded in this baseline; fail only on new findings. A
single file is free; a directory unions every .json baseline inside it (an
org\-shared accepted set) and requires a Team license. Without this flag,
~/.config/skarn/baseline.json is applied automatically when it exists
(suppression only; \-\-baseline\-create and \-\-baseline\-merge targets stay
explicit).
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\-create\fR
Write the current findings to the \-\-baseline file as the accepted set and
exit.
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\-merge\fR \fIfile\fR
Consolidate the \-\-baseline input (a file or a directory of per\-member
baselines) into one distributable file and exit. Requires a Team license.
.TP
\fB\-\-audit\-log\fR \fIfile\fR
Append a hash\-chained record of this scan (timestamp, policy, finding counts,
verdict; no secrets) to this local log; detects in\-place edits and reordering
of recorded history. Requires a Team license.
.TP
\fB\-\-audit\-verify\fR \fIfile\fR
Verify an audit log's hash chain and exit (no scan). Exits non\-zero if the
chain is broken. Needs no license.
.SS "Assess options"
The zero\-config machine\-scan wedge: scan every AI coding session on the
machine and print a friendly risk summary, no flags and no license. \-o writes
a shareable redacted report (HTML or Markdown); \-\-json emits the redacted
scan for scripting. The scan is scoped by the same filters as check.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-out\fR \fIfile\fR
Write a self\-contained, redacted share report to this file; .md or .markdown
renders Markdown, any other extension renders HTML.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit the redacted scan as JSON (the same shape as the serve /api/scan view)
for scripting, instead of the friendly summary.
.TP
\fB\-\-offline\fR
Disable all network access. Assess is already egress\-free; this is the
belt\-and\-suspenders guarantee.
.TP
\fB\-\-hours\fR \fIn\fR
Scan window in hours; 0 (the default) scans every session on the machine. The
default is 0.
.TP
\fB\-\-severity\fR \fIlevel\fR
Only summarize findings at or above this severity; the default (low) scans
everything and curates the display. One of: low, medium, high, critical. The
default is low.
.TP
\fB\-\-cli\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict the scan to one assistant's sessions. One of: claude, gemini, codex,
cursor, copilot.
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIpath\fR
Restrict the scan to sessions under this project path.
.TP
\fB\-\-session\fR \fIid\fR
Assess a single session by id.
.TP
\fB\-\-rules\fR \fIpath\fR
Load a custom TOML rules file in addition to the bundled rules.
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\fR \fIpath\fR
Suppress findings recorded in this accepted\-findings baseline. Without this
flag, ~/.config/skarn/baseline.json is applied automatically when it exists. A
directory of baselines requires a Team license; assess never gates, so an
unlicensed directory degrades to a warning and an unsuppressed scan.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR
Disable colored output.
.SS "Baseline options"
Interactive baseline triage. audit walks each new finding and records a
true/false\-positive decision with a reason into the committed baseline;
accept records a single false\-positive fingerprint non\-interactively. The
audit scan is scoped by the same filters as check.
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fItext\fR
Reason recorded with an accepted finding (audit prompts for one; accept takes
it as a flag).
.TP
\fB\-\-identity\fR \fIid\fR
Override the accepted\-by identity stamped on the decision; defaults to
$SKARN_IDENTITY.
.TP
\fB\-\-cli\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict the audit scan to one assistant's sessions. One of: claude, gemini,
codex, cursor, copilot.
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIpath\fR
Restrict the audit scan to sessions under this project path.
.TP
\fB\-\-session\fR \fIid\fR
Audit a single session by id.
.TP
\fB\-\-rules\fR \fIpath\fR
Load a custom TOML rules file for the audit scan in addition to the bundled
rules.
.TP
\fB\-\-hours\fR \fIn\fR
Audit scan window in hours; 0 means no time limit. The default is 720.
.TP
\fB\-\-severity\fR \fIlevel\fR
Only audit findings at or above this severity. One of: low, medium, high,
critical. The default is medium.
.SS "Guard options"
Control the real\-time pre\-execution hook used for editor/agent integration
(Claude Code, Cursor, Codex CLI, and Copilot CLI).
.TP
\fB\-\-guard\-mode\fR \fImode\fR
audit reports only; enforce emits a deny or ask verdict. Enforce needs a
license; an unlicensed guard runs audit\-only. One of: audit, enforce. The
default is audit.
.TP
\fB\-\-strict\fR
Also gate Read operations (credential\-file reconnaissance).
.TP
\fB\-\-agent\fR \fIname\fR
Hook host (default: auto\-detect from the event shape; pass codex explicitly
for Codex CLI, which shares Claude's event names, and copilot explicitly for
Copilot CLI, whose Claude\-compat payloads are indistinguishable from
Claude's). With \-\-self\-test, all (the default) tests the claude, cursor,
and codex adapters. One of: claude, cursor, codex, copilot, all.
.TP
\fB\-\-self\-test\fR
Verify the guard wiring instead of reading an event: feed two synthetic
fixture events (one benign, one carrying a fake credential) through each
adapter's real code path and report the verdicts, latency, and a guard\-log
write probe. The fake credential is only ever printed redacted. Exit 0 when
every adapter passes, 1 otherwise; works unlicensed (audit semantics).
.SS "Setup options"
Guided onboarding: detect installed AI coding agents (Claude Code, Cursor,
Codex CLI), merge the skarn guard hook into each one's native config in audit
mode, and prove the wiring with the guard self\-test. Existing foreign hooks
are never touched; every modified file is backed up first
(*.skarn\-bak.<timestamp>) and rewritten atomically; a target file that does
not parse as JSON is refused, never overwritten. On a terminal it runs an
interactive wizard with default\-yes prompts; \-\-yes accepts everything,
\-\-print writes nothing.
.TP
\fB\-\-agent\fR \fIname\fR
Target agents, repeatable or comma\-separated. Default: all detected (an agent
is detected when its config directory exists or its CLI is on PATH); naming an
agent explicitly also targets it when undetected. One of: claude, cursor,
codex, all.
.TP
\fB\-\-mode\fR \fImode\fR
Guard mode written into the hook commands. Audit records would\-be verdicts to
the guard log without blocking; start there, review the log, then flip to
enforce. Enforce needs a license to actually block; an unlicensed guard runs
audit\-only regardless. One of: audit, enforce. The default is audit.
.TP
\fB\-\-strict\fR
Add \-\-strict to the generated guard commands (also gates Read operations;
fail\-closed semantics on Codex).
.TP
\fB\-\-log\fR \fIpath\fR
Guard log path wired into the hook commands as the SKARN_GUARD_LOG prefix;
pass none to disable logging. The Codex commandWindows form carries no prefix
(set the variable in the environment on Windows). The default is
~/.cache/skarn/guard\-<agent>\-audit.jsonl.
.TP
\fB\-\-command\fR \fIpath\fR
The guard executable invoked by the hook commands. The default resolves this
binary's absolute path (preferring the stable Homebrew <prefix>/bin symlink
over the versioned Cellar path) because GUI\-launched hosts like Cursor do not
inherit brew's PATH; pass skarn to opt into PATH\-relative. The default is
this binary's absolute path.
.TP
\fB\-\-scope\fR \fIscope\fR
Write user\-level configs (~/.claude/settings.json, ~/.cursor/hooks.json,
~/.codex/hooks.json) or project\-level ones (./.claude, ./.cursor, ./.codex
relative to the working directory). One of: user, project. The default is
user.
.TP
\fB\-\-print\fR
Print the final per\-agent config to stdout and write nothing; the
paste\-it\-yourself and fleet\-template path.
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fIfmt\fR
With \-\-print: json emits one {agent, path, content} envelope per agent for
machine consumption. One of: text, json. The default is text.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Non\-interactive: accept every prompt's default. Required when running without
a terminal.
.TP
\fB\-\-update\fR
Only touch agents whose config already carries skarn entries; the
audit\-to\-enforce flip is skarn setup \-\-update \-\-mode enforce.
.TP
\fB\-\-remove\fR
Surgically remove skarn\-owned hook entries, dropping any matcher groups and
event arrays they leave empty. Foreign hooks are untouched, the file itself is
never deleted, and backups from earlier runs are kept.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print a machine\-readable result summary (per agent: path, action, backup,
self\-test result) instead of the text report.
.SS "Doctor options"
Health, wiring, and dead\-hook detection. Doctor runs one named check per line
\- the binary and its path, the license, each detected agent's hook config,
whether each wired hook command still resolves to an executable, whether the
wiring matches what this version of skarn writes, the guard log's freshness,
the session stores it can enumerate, the guard self\-test, and the cache
directory \- and every warning or failure names the exact command that clears
it. Check ids (binary, license, agent_config_<agent>, guard_command_<agent>,
config_drift_<agent>, guard_log_<agent>, self_test_<agent>, session_stores,
cache_dir) are a stable, greppable contract for fleet scripts. A failure exits
1; a warning never does; \-\-json is a data mode and always exits 0. Doctor is
hermetic: it makes no network call, shells out to nothing, and enumerates the
session stores without reading a session's content.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Add detail to every check: the paths searched, each wired hook command, the
per\-tool session counts, and the resolved Homebrew path.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit the full structured result: {version, checks[{id, status, detail, fix,
notes}], summary{pass, warn, fail}}. Always exits 0.
.SS "Serve options"
Control the localhost web UI. Access requires the per\-run token embedded in
the URL the command prints at startup; opening that URL sets a session cookie,
and scripts may pass it as a token= query parameter instead.
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fIn\fR
Port to listen on; 0 asks the OS to assign one. Always binds 127.0.0.1 only.
The default is 7777.
.TP
\fB\-\-rules\fR \fIpath\fR
Load a custom TOML rules file in addition to the bundled rules; the web
Security scan then also surfaces its findings.
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\fR \fIpath\fR
Suppress findings recorded in this accepted\-findings baseline; the Security
view hides them by default behind a show/hide toggle. Without this flag,
~/.config/skarn/baseline.json is applied automatically when it exists. A
directory of baselines (the org form) requires a Team license.
.SS "Global options"
Available on a bare invocation.
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version and exit.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help and exit.
.SS "Recent options"
List and filter recent sessions.
.TP
\fB\-\-cli\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict to one assistant's sessions. One of: claude, gemini, codex, cursor,
copilot.
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIpath\fR
Restrict to sessions whose project path contains this substring.
.TP
\fB\-\-worktree\fR \fIpath\fR
Restrict to sessions whose working directory is under this path.
.TP
\fB\-\-since\fR \fIdate\fR
Lower bound of the time window; takes priority over \-\-hours.
.TP
\fB\-\-until\fR \fIdate\fR
Upper bound of the time window.
.TP
\fB\-\-tz\-offset\fR \fIminutes\fR
Timezone offset in minutes for date grouping and display. The default is 0.
.TP
\fB\-\-hours\fR \fIn\fR
Time window in hours; 0 means no limit. The default is 24.
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fIfmt\fR
Output format. One of: text, json, csv. The default is text.
.TP
\fB\-\-include\-empty\fR
Include sessions with no user or assistant messages (off by default).
.SS "Messages options"
Show one session's messages. The session is a positional id or \-\-session;
